<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Persian Cossack Brigade</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1879">1879</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cossack Brigade</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> was a cavalry force formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> in 1879 on the model of the Russian Cossacks and commanded by Russian officers. It became the shah's most effective military unit and bombarded the Majlis in 1908 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah</a>. Under its Iranian officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan</a> it carried out the coup of February 1921, and it was then merged into a new national army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/persian-cossack-brigade.jpg" alt="Persian Cossack Brigade" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Persian_Cossack_Brigade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Naser al-Din Shah had admired the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> who escorted him across Russian Armenia on his journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1878–1879, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tiflis</a> he asked Grand Duke Mikhail for Russian help in raising such a unit; Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> approved. Unlike true Cossacks, who served in return for grants of land, the brigade was recruited from volunteers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>. Lieutenant-Colonel Aleksey Domantovich formed it with 400 Caucasian muhajirs, descendants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circassians">Circassians</a> and Transcaucasian Muslims who had fled Russian rule. He enforced discipline and paid the men on time, but the Russian minister in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> saw him as a rival and had him removed in 1881.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under his successors the brigade shrank through budget cuts, desertion and cholera to just 150 men. Colonel Kosagovskij revived it, ending the muhajirs' privileges after their mutiny of 1895. When Naser al-Din was assassinated on 1 May 1896 the brigade held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>, and on 7 June 1896 it escorted the heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozaffar-ad-din-shah-qajar">Mozaffar ad-Din Shah</a>, into the capital. By 1903 it had about 1,500 men and was seen by British observers as under the direct control of the Russian legation.</p><p>Under Colonel Liakhov it shelled the parliament for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah</a>, but forces led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sattar-khan">Sattar Khan</a> and Yeprem Khan retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and the shah abdicated. In 1916 the Russians enlarged it to a division of about 8,000 men, which fought Ottoman troops. After the Russian Revolution Britain paid its wages from December 1917 and most of the Russian officers left.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 14 January 1921 the British general Ironside gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan</a>, who had joined the brigade at sixteen, command of the whole force. Reza Khan's men from Qazvin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, 3,000 to 4,000 strong, then seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> in a largely bloodless coup. Merged with the gendarmerie into a new army of 40,000, the brigade became its core, and its former officers held key posts as Reza Khan made himself shah and founded the Pahlavi dynasty. Soviet historians long portrayed it simply as a tool of Russian imperialism, which the historians Uzi Rabi and Nugzar Ter-Oganov say left much about it unstudied.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Persian Cossack Brigade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Persian Cossack Brigade</b>, founded in 1879 under Russian officers, became the most effective unit and was used in 1908 to bombard the Majlis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Reza Khan, an officer of the <b>Persian Cossack Brigade</b>, seized power in a coup in 1921 and became prime minister in 1923.</blockquote><a href="/state/pahlavi-iran">Pahlavi Iran</a></li></ul></section>
